  "title": "Puja",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "About 8% of Bangladeshis are Hindu, and **দুর্গাপূজা** (*durgapuja*) is their biggest **উৎসব** (festival). It falls in autumn (September or October) and lasts several days. Temporary pavilions (*mondop*) each hold a clay **প্রতিমা**, an image of the goddess Durga, and people of all faiths come to see them.\n\nOn the last day, **বিজয়া** (*Bijoya Doshomi*, a public holiday in Bangladesh), the images are immersed in a river or pond. Afterwards people greet each other with **শুভ বিজয়া** and share sweets. With Hindu friends, **নমস্কার** is the natural hello.\n\n**পূজা** means 'worship' as well as the festival. Endings: **পূজায়** 'at Puja', **মন্দিরে** 'to / at the temple'.\n\nA popular saying: *dhormo jar jar, utshob shobar*, 'religion is each person's own; festivals belong to everyone'. **ঈদ আর পূজা আমাদের উৎসব** 'Eid and Puja are our festivals.'\n\n**Kolkata:** spoken **পুজো** (*pujo*), **দুর্গাপুজো**; Durga Puja is West Bengal's biggest festival.",
